  "step_3": "The decision reason relies on computing an aspiration level from all outcomes and comparing each outcome to it, then favoring the lottery with a higher chance of exceeding this level. The individual does consider the similarity of the outcomes (2500 and 2400), which could implicitly relate to a central reference, but there is no indication that they computed or used a mean of all outcomes as a threshold. Instead, the individual focuses on the higher winning probability of B, given the small difference in outcomes. This reasoning aligns more with a probability-maximizing heuristic under outcome similarity, not with evaluating outcomes relative to an aspiration level. There is no evidence that the individual calculated or used positive differences from a computed mean outcome. While both the reason and the individual favor Lottery B, the justification process differs fundamentally. The similarity in outcomes might loosely relate to the aspiration concept, but the absence of any reference to a computed threshold or comparison of deviations makes it unlikely the specific reason was used.",
